  "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/46263b1e25bc520881d590ccab503ce0d6cd33e91288e81f1d678cde9a641214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Brooke and the library,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Alex and the library,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Alex and Brooke.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{4}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 9+16 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(6) & =2(5)(6) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+48 & =60 \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brooke's speed is $2\\,\\text{km/h}.$",
